WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
The linear-type cast full lines of type from molten metal, turning hours of hand-setting

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.520
a newspaper page into minutes.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:10.400
This is First Principles Daily, episode 97.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:16.020
Newspapers once paid teams of compositors to pick individual metal letters from wooden

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.700
cases and assemble them line by line.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.760
That process could consume most of a shift for a single page.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:30.160
Ottmar Mergan-Thawler's 1886 machine accepted keystrokes, assembled reusable brass matrices

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:36.320
into a justified line, and poured molten metal to produce a single solid slug ready for the press.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:39.900
The change did not merely speed up an existing craft.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:45.060
It replaced the assembly of separate pieces with the direct formation of the finished line

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:50.800
from raw alloy, where a page once required repeated handling of dozens of separate swords

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:56.260
per line, the machine formed the entire line as one piece whose surface matched the printing

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.540
requirement exactly.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:02.820
The shift exposed how much of the prior cost had been locked into the habit of treating

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:08.260
each character as an independent, reusable object that must be selected and positioned

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:09.000
by hand.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.280
Printers had followed the same sequence since Gutenberg.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.300
Each character was a physical object stored in a case, selected my hand, and locked into

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:18.800
a form.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.180
The convention rested on the assumption that type must remain reusable as discrete units,

00:01:24.580 --> 00:01:28.460
so that pages could be broken down and the letters returned to their compartments.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.240
That assumption carried forward unchanged for centuries because every shop already owned

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.460
cases, composing sticks, and skilled labor trained in the same motions.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.600
The finished cost of a page therefore consisted almost entirely of wages multiplied by the

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.300
hours required to achieve the required accuracy and justification.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:55.380
A compositor might set 1500 to 2,000 characters in an hour under good conditions, yet the work

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.140
still demanded constant checking for spelling, spacing, and alignment.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:04.520
Because the letters themselves were durable and relatively inexpensive to cast in advance,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.900
the industry treated the high labor content as an unavoidable feature of printing rather

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.820
than a variable to be engineered away.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.160
Shops competed by hiring more compositors or extending shifts.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.780
They never questioned whether the letter by letter model itself could be discarded.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:26.440
The result was a stable equilibrium in which newspapers accepted slow production and

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.580
high staffing as the normal price of daily publication.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.900
Any attempt to alter the unit of work would have required new equipment and new skills,

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:40.480
so the existing supply chain of foundry type and trained hands continued to define what

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:41.520
counted as feasible.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.180
The physical durability of the individual sorts reinforced the assumption that the bottleneck

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:52.160
was simply the speed of human fingers rather than the decision to keep the characters separate

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.220
at every stage.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.760
Murgenthaler asked what physical steps were strictly necessary to produce a printable line

00:02:58.760 --> 00:02:59.320
of text.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.360
The raw requirement was a raised image of the letters in the correct order and alignment.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.840
Everything else was inherited habit.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:12.260
He therefore designed a keyboard that selected individual brass matrices from a magazine.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:17.280
Assembled them into a line and justified that line with expandable space bands.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:23.900
Once the line was complete, molten type metal, an alloy of lead, antimony, and tin was

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:29.160
pumped against the matrices to cast a single solid slug whose face carried the entire line.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:34.740
After casting, the matrices were automatically returned to their channels while the slug

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:35.620
moved to the galley.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.040
Each of these steps attacked a different component of the old labor cost.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.520
The keyboard removed the physical reaching and sorting of letters.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:48.920
The reusable matrices eliminated the need to distribute individual sorts after printing.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:55.060
The casting step collapsed justification, alignment, and line formation into one operation

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.560
performed by the machine rather than the compositor's hands.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:04.100
The critical engineering trade-off was maintaining precise matrix alignment and metal temperature

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:07.760
so that every slug emerged with sharp, consistent faces.

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:12.320
Without that reliability, the machine would have produced more waste than it saved.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:17.700
A rough magic wand estimate for the finished slug begins with the commodity price of the

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:18.660
type metal itself.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:24.560
That price sat at roughly a few cents per ounce in the late 19th century, plus the

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.680
amortized cost of the brass matrices.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.860
Under the old method, the same line of text embodied many minutes of skilled wages.

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:38.480
The idiot index expressed as labor costs divided by material cost, therefore sat at a very

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:43.800
high ratio. The dominant expense was the repeated manual handling rather than the metal.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:49.380
The lino-type reduced that ratio by substituting machine-controlled casting for most of the

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:55.000
handling steps. One objection sometimes raised is that the machine still required an operator

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.760
and periodic maintenance. Yet, those remaining human tasks were now applied to a process whose

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:05.400
output rate had increased dramatically rather than to the selection of each character.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:10.620
Another potential limit is that the brass matrices themselves had to be manufactured

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.220
to tight tolerances, but their cost could be spread across thousands of castings, moving

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.020
the expense from per line labor into a smaller per-use fraction.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.780
The redesign succeeded because it kept the matrices as the reusable intermediate, while

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:30.460
discarding the assumption that the final printed line must be built from separate pre-cast

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:36.200
pieces. Newspapers that adopted the machine could produce pages several times faster and

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:41.840
with far fewer compositors per addition. This lowered the cost per page enough to support larger

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:47.500
papers and more frequent additions. The new idiot index fell because the material cost of the

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:53.720
slug remained low, while the labor per line dropped sharply. Yet, the process still required molten

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:59.980
metal, constant temperature control, and eventual distribution of the slugs themselves. So it never

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:05.760
reached the absolute floor set by the alloy price alone. Later technologies such as photo-type

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:11.260
setting and digital composition bypassed metal entirely. They showed that even the improved

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:15.640
lino-type design contained further layers of physical assumption that could be removed

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:21.820
once the constraint of relief printing loosened. The machine also introduced new dependencies on

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:27.280
consistent alloy supply and on the durability of the brass matrices under repeated use,

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:33.400
both of which had to be managed to preserve the cost advantage. In practice, the largest remaining

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:39.360
costs after adoption were no longer the selection of individual letters, but the preparation and

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:45.400
upkeep of the machine itself. That narrower but still significant slice of the original labor bird

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:51.700
in remained. One principle is that any process whose largest cost is repeated human selection of

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:58.400
small identical units is announcing an opportunity to form those units in place rather than assemble them.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:05.320
A second principle is that reusable intermediate tools, such as the brass matrices, can be retained

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:11.020
while the final output is produced in a single forming step that matches the physics of the end use.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:17.180
The same logic that turned individual letters into cast slugs now appears in industries that

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:22.940
still treat custom fabrication as inevitable. The first signal that someone is applying it will be

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:28.460
a working prototype that produces the finished article directly from raw stock instead of from

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:34.480
pre-made components. That's first principles daily for today. If this changed how you see what

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:47.160
things cost, share it with someone who depreciated and subscribe wherever you listen. I'm Patrick

